  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Consider the cubic polynomial $P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d$ with $a,b,c,d$ all integers.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q10-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that if $d$ is odd, then $P(x)$ has no even integer roots.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No even integer roots.\n\nFor even $k$, $k(ak^2+bk+c)$ is even and cannot equal the odd integer $-d$.",
          "worked": "Suppose an even integer $k$ is a root. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}P(k)&=0\\\\ak^3+bk^2+ck+d&=0\\\\ak^3+bk^2+ck&=-d\\\\k(ak^2+bk+c)&=-d.\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is even because $k$ is even and $ak^2+bk+c$ is an integer. The right-hand side is odd because $d$ is odd. This contradiction proves that $P(x)$ has no even integer roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q10-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that if $a$ is odd and $b,c,d$ are all even, then $P(x)$ has no odd integer roots.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No odd integer roots.\n\nFor odd $k$, $bk^2+ck+d$ is even, whereas $-ak^3$ is odd.",
          "worked": "Suppose an odd integer $k$ is a root. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}P(k)&=0\\\\ak^3+bk^2+ck+d&=0\\\\bk^2+ck+d&=-ak^3.\\end{align*}$$\nThe left-hand side is even because $b,c,d$ are even. The right-hand side is odd because both $a$ and $k$ are odd. This contradiction proves that $P(x)$ has no odd integer roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      }
    ]
  },
